// B4 (2026-09-30), the control: a callee declared `!Thread` keeps its own
// frame's schedule. `both` proves it performs no `Thread`, so a caller's
// `parallel_compose` never reaches its fanout — the branches run in the
// root instance (`thread_id()` is the substrate's own op, 0 there) and the
// negation holds where the demand would have spawned. The same on the
// prior boot, where nothing crossed a call at all: this leg pins the
// negation as the demand's gate, not the mechanism.
// Expected exit: 1.
fn both() with !Thread = (thread_id()) >< (thread_id())
fn main() = match (both()) ~> parallel_compose {
  (a, b) => if a > 0 && b > 0 { 14 } else { 1 },
}
